WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.360 --> 00:00:04.260
Next month, an international team of astronomers

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:06.760
will harness the power of radio telescopes

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:09.560
across the globe to achieve the resolution

00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:13.260
of a single Earth-sized dish in order to take a picture

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:15.420
of the black hole at the center of our galaxy.

00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:20.440
Imaging a black hole is a formidable challenge

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:22.800
and not just because black holes have such

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:25.120
intense gravity that they emit nothing,

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:26.600
not even light.

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:29.100
They are also surprisingly small

00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:32.840
The Milky Way’s black hole, known as Sagittarius A*,

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:37.600
is calculated to contain the mass of 4 million suns

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:39.140
but its event horizon,

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:40.220
the point of no return

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:41.740
for anything approaching the black hole

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:45.360
is just 24 million kilometers across,

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:47.960
around 1/2 the distance between Mercury and the sun

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:50.280
To see something so small

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:52.620
from 26,000 light-years away

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:56.020
requires a telescope dish of global dimensions

00:00:56.540 --> 00:00:59.480
and that is what the Event Horizon Telescope

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:01.280
is aiming to produce.

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:03.880
Because of the difficulty of coordinating simultaneous

00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:07.460
observations at eight facilities on four continents

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:10.100
plus Hawaii, the team can observe for only

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:13.640
five nights during a 10-night window in early April.

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:16.680
The amounts of data gathered are so huge that

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:18.720
they have to be recorded on hard drives and

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:19.980
flown back for processing

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:21.320
at two centers

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:23.720
It may be early 2018 or even longer before

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:25.580
they know whether they’ve succeeded.

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:28.800
The team hopes they will see a bright ring of light

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:30.500
made up of photons

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:32.220
orbiting close to the event horizon

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:34.120
--with a dark disk in its center

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:36.700
The ring will likely be brighter on one side

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:38.340
where photons are boosted by the rotation

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:39.680
of the black hole.

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:42.380
Will there be structure in the matter

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:44.520
swirling around the event horizon?

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:47.580
Will the images reveal how black holes power

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:50.400
the massive jets that often blast from their poles?

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:53.900
The galactic center has a history of surprising us:

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:56.980
we may see things no one expected in the

00:01:56.980 --> 00:01:59.800
very first images of its black hole.

